  const mock = "MOCK-TOKEN-ABC123";
  console.log(leet(mock)); // M0CK-70K3N-48C123

Reversibility caveat
- If you try to decode back, you must know the exact substitutions. Decoding can be lossy in practice because digits can exist naturally and because letters like E or O could have originated as the same digits in some contexts.
- For tested integrity, you can confirm the structure is preserved (e.g., hyphens and token segments remain in place) while content is non-readable. If you need true reversibility, use a trackable mapping or a reversible cipher and store the key/mapping securely.

